    "items": [
      {
        "text": "Linda Sánchez has served in the U.S. House of Representatives since 2003. She worked as a labor and employment attorney and served as executive director of the Orange County Central Labor Council, AFL-CIO. She serves on the House Committee on Ways and Means and is the ranking member of its Trade Subcommittee. She also serves on a subcommittee related to healthcare.",
        "cites": [1, 2, 3],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "Linda Sánchez has served in the U.S. House of Representatives since 2003.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "She worked as a labor and employment attorney and served as executive director of the Orange County Central Labor Council, AFL-CIO.",
            "cites": [2]
          },
          {
            "text": "She serves on the House Committee on Ways and Means and is the ranking member of its Trade Subcommittee.",
            "cites": [3]
          },
          {
            "text": "She also serves on a subcommittee related to healthcare.",
            "cites": [1]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "In 2009 she introduced the Gender Equity in Health Premiums Act, which barred insurers from charging women more than men and became part of the Affordable Care Act. Her website states that a bill she sponsored to simplify paperwork for small businesses offering retirement plans was signed into law. On housing affordability, she points to a recent bipartisan bill that would let builders immediately deduct construction costs rather than spread deductions over nearly 30 years, which she says would address a lack of multifamily rental housing in Southern California. She supports restoring tax credits that reduced health insurance costs under the Affordable Care Act and wants to close a loophole that raises premiums when one spouse ages into Medicare. She has introduced bills to expand Medicare coverage for adult day care and reform the hospice benefit to improve care and prevent fraud.",
        "cites": [3, 4, 1],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "In 2009 she introduced the Gender Equity in Health Premiums Act, which barred insurers from charging women more than men and became part of the Affordable Care Act.",
            "cites": [3]
          },
          {
            "text": "Her website states that a bill she sponsored to simplify paperwork for small businesses offering retirement plans was signed into law.",
            "cites": [4]
          },
          {
            "text": "On housing affordability, she points to a recent bipartisan bill that would let builders immediately deduct construction costs rather than spread deductions over nearly 30 years, which she says would address a lack of multifamily rental housing in Southern California.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "She supports restoring tax credits that reduced health insurance costs under the Affordable Care Act and wants to close a loophole that raises premiums when one spouse ages into Medicare.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "She has introduced bills to expand Medicare coverage for adult day care and reform the hospice benefit to improve care and prevent fraud.",
            "cites": [1]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "She sponsored the PFAS Cleanup Act, which her office says would establish a tax on the manufacturing of PFAS chemicals to fund a tax credit for water agencies to remove them. In September 2026 she voted against a resolution to disapprove California’s harbor craft pollution standards. Her campaign says she is fighting to lower prices.",
        "cites": [25, 5, 2]
      }
    ]
  },
